A||||9||BC-GT||Distributor, pick-up coil, timing, and glowing manifolds||||1||
Z||000000||BC-GT||12-11-2001||08:24 PM||||Ok, I think I got enough <B>key</B> words in the header, here's my problem/question. I replaced the pickup coil and a few other things the other day, dropped the distibutor back in and it fired up first crank and ran good. Thought all was well but now my manifolds are glowing... <B>So, is it possible that my distributor could be in wrong and the car can still run good?</B> They never glowed before. I know I can pull the #1 plug and realign the distributor, but it's more work pulling a plug and finding TDC than pulling the bloody distributor.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.imagestation.com/picture/sraid27/pe8dae45bb8bf084e93628ae50c338631/fe1839db.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||12-11-2001||08:31 PM||||Oh toooooooo easy......been there done that a million times. You placed your dist in wrong and your timing is way off. Believe me, like I said, Ive done it lots and lots of times. It can be a headache now though. <BR>Try the normal advance/retard the timing by turning the cap...if that doesnt work, you were off by a tooth or two when placing your dist back in. <BR>As a matter of fact, I did that on my 87 Gt, I was sooo frustrated that I couldnt get it right that I finally to it to the Fiero Factory (Huntsville). Jeremy (the mechanic) said he had never seen manifolds glow as much as mine did. He said they were bright red from the head all the way down as far as he could see. Needless to say he fixed it though. I hate timing these things!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Good luck!<p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 12-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||BC-GT||12-11-2001||08:57 PM||||Stupid pick-up coils, you think they would make one you could drop on from the top eh! Anyway, yeah that's probably my problem, just the way it started and ran so well, made me wonder. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||GTDude||12-11-2001||09:21 PM||||Never seen it happen on a Fiero...but i've seen it happen on lots of 6000's and firebirds.  Can almost guarantee that the timing is off unless the engine got super rich for some reason....nahhhh.  Set your timing correctly and keep kicking ass.<P>GTDude||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||12-12-2001||10:46 AM||||BC-GT, while timing <I>could</I> be the problem I noticed that you said it fired right up and ran fine - if you had set the distributor in off a tooth or two it probably would not have started at all unless you turned the distributor one direction or the other. <P>There <I>are</I> other things that can cause manifolds to glow such as a massive vacuum leak or a malfunctioning fuel injector.......  in this case I would lean to the vacuum leak theory -- are you sure that all of the vacuum lines are connected and not damaged (most of the time one or two are disconnected in order to make distributor removal easier)?<P>This is just a thought but, unless the distributor was placed in 180 degrees off the car would hesitate to start and you would have recognized a timing problem right off the bat...||||reg||1
Z||000005||BC-GT||12-12-2001||11:47 AM||||Thanks tgowens, the motor was just rebuilt with new ACDelco injectors(not rebuilt) installed, so I'm gonna kinda rule out those, as far as the vacuum lines, I just installed Rodney's steel lines, so I'll double check them. <B>now, what would be the result *if* the distributor was in 180 degrees off?</B><BR>BTW, the idle seemed fine(not high) and steady, though I didn't run it too long.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||tesmith66||12-12-2001||03:42 PM||||There's also the EGR tube. Did you lean on it or bump it or look at it wrong? They can get VERY fragile in their old age.<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000007||K2m4||12-13-2001||11:57 AM||||BUMP<BR>Im curious to hear the answer of BC last question!<BR><P>------------------<BR>Kerm<BR>86 GT<BR>86 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000008||BC-GT||12-16-2001||06:42 PM||||OK, it was the timing, after I grounded the computer and tried to start it again it barely ran(~the computer over compensating without grounding?), adjusted it after grounding the computer and all is fine. On a side note, after I put the new fuel pressure regulator in <B> all my stumbling/hesitation is also gone...</B><BR>WoooooooooHoooooooo <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||GTDude||12-16-2001||09:03 PM||||Always good to hear of a problem solved.  Glad you were able to fix it...and merry christmas.<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
